Reasoning: Delivery falling to zero while consumers stay connected usually means the prefetch window is full of unacked deliveries — the broker stops pushing until something is acked. Check the queue counters first.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_queues|> {"vhost": "/payments", "columns": ["name", "messages_ready", "messages_unacknowledged", "consumers"]}
System Response: payments.capture  ready=5480  unacked=1600  consumers=16
Recovery reasoning: 1600 unacked across 16 consumers is exactly 100 per consumer — a full prefetch window on every channel. Something stopped acking around the deploy. Check the consumer application logs.
<|call:tail_app_logs|> {"service": "payments-consumer", "lines": 200}
System Response: 200 lines: repeated "gateway connection reset by peer" errors from card-gateway-svc with stack traces in capture_handler.py.
Recovery reasoning: Gateway resets look like the culprit, but the timestamps run 08:03-09:12 — almost three hours before the 11:40 stall — and stop entirely afterwards; the tail picked up a rotated segment, and nothing in it is contemporaneous with the incident. The gateway is a dead end. Inspect the consumers' channel configuration instead.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_consumers|> {"vhost": "/payments", "queue": "payments.capture"}
System Response: 16 consumers, ack_required=true, prefetch_count=100 each, channels opened 11:41-11:43 (matches the v2.4 rollout).
Recovery reasoning: Manual ack, prefetch 100, channels born at the deploy. v2.4 moved capture logic to an async handler and the basic.ack call stayed in the old synchronous path that no longer runs, so each consumer processed 100 messages and stalled. Roll the fleet back.
<|call:restart_consumer_fleet|> {"service": "payments-consumer", "image": "payments-consumer:v2.3"}
System Response: 16 pods restarted on v2.3; channels reopened; unacked 1600 -> 0 in 40s; ack rate 210/s.
Resolution: Root cause: v2.4 dropped the basic.ack call when the handler went async, exhausting the 100-message prefetch window on all 16 consumers and stalling delivery. Rollback restored acking; the 5480-message backlog drained in about half a minute and delivery is steady at 190/s.

Reasoning: Publishers blocking without errors while consumers keep draining is the signature of a broker resource alarm — the broker sends connection.blocked and stops reading from publishing connections but lets consumers run. Check cluster health first.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_get|> {"path": "/api/overview"}
System Response: cluster mq-prod: mem_used avg 3.1GiB, mem_limit 6.4GiB per node, no alarm banner; rates: deliver 4.1k/s, publish 0/s.
Recovery reasoning: The overview averages memory across the three nodes and its alarm banner reflects only the node serving the API request — one alarmed node vanishes into a healthy 3.1GiB mean. But publish at exactly 0/s cluster-wide says blocking is real. Query each node directly.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_status|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-prod-2"}
System Response: rabbit@mq-prod-2: mem_used 6.6GiB, vm_memory_high_watermark 0.4 (limit 6.4GiB), alarms: [memory], 118 connections in state blocked.
Recovery reasoning: mq-prod-2 is 200MiB over its watermark and raised the memory alarm, which blocks publishers cluster-wide. Find what holds the memory — likely a backlog sitting in RAM on a classic queue.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_queues|> {"vhost": "/orders", "columns": ["name", "messages", "memory"], "sort": "memory", "limit": 3}
System Response: orders.analytics ready=4200000 memory=5.9GiB (classic, hosted on rabbit@mq-prod-2); next largest queue 41MiB.
Recovery reasoning: A 4.2M-message classic queue keeping its backlog in RAM accounts for 5.9 of the 6.6GiB; its consumer is a nightly batch job. Page it to disk with a lazy policy instead of purging data.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_set_policy|> {"vhost": "/orders", "name": "analytics-lazy", "pattern": "^orders.analytics$", "definition": {"queue-mode": "lazy"}, "apply-to": "queues"}
System Response: policy set; queue paging to disk; mem_used 6.6GiB -> 2.4GiB over 90s; memory alarm cleared; connection.unblocked sent to 118 connections.
Resolution: Root cause: orders.analytics accumulated 4.2M messages in memory on mq-prod-2 and tripped the 0.4 vm_memory_high_watermark; the alarm blocked every publisher cluster-wide while consumers kept draining, which is why depths were falling. Lazy mode paged the backlog to disk; publish recovered to 3.9k/s and checkout p99 is back to 180ms.

Reasoning: Publish and deliver balanced at 12k/s with only 40/s of real work smells like a message cycle rather than a client flood. Still, first find who is publishing at that rate.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_connections|> {"vhost": "/orders", "columns": ["client", "publish_rate"]}
System Response: 9 connections: retry-worker x8 publishing 0/s, orders-api x1 publishing 38/s. No connection above 40/s.
Recovery reasoning: Clients account for 38/s of the 12k/s entering the queue — the missing 11.96k/s cannot come from any AMQP connection, so it is broker-internal traffic, and the only internal publisher is dead-lettering. Inspect the queue arguments and where the DLX routes.
<|call:inspect_queue_args|> {"vhost": "/orders", "queue": "orders.retry"}
System Response: x-dead-letter-exchange=orders.dlx, no x-dead-letter-routing-key, no TTL. Bindings on orders.dlx: orders.retry bound with key retry.#.
Recovery reasoning: Rejected messages dead-letter to orders.dlx, which routes them straight back into orders.retry under the original routing key — a zero-delay DLX cycle. The x-death header should show the loop count. Sample a message.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_get|> {"path": "/api/queues/%2Forders/orders.retry/get", "count": 1, "requeue": true}
System Response: 1 message: x-death count=8341, first-death-reason=rejected, routing key retry.payment, payload order 88412 created 09:14.
Recovery reasoning: One order rejected 8341 times since 09:14 — the cycle is confirmed. Break it by overriding the dead-letter routing key to a parking key that only the DLQ is bound to.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_set_policy|> {"vhost": "/orders", "name": "retry-dlx-park", "pattern": "^orders.retry$", "definition": {"dead-letter-exchange": "orders.dlx", "dead-letter-routing-key": "parked"}, "apply-to": "queues"}
System Response: policy applied. orders.dlq (bound to orders.dlx with key parked) now receiving 6 msg/s; orders.retry publish 12k/s -> 44/s; node CPU 92% -> 31%.
Resolution: Root cause: orders.retry dead-lettered rejects into orders.dlx with no routing-key override, and the retry.# binding re-matched the original key, cycling every reject back into the queue instantly. The parked dead-letter-routing-key sends rejects to orders.dlq; loop traffic stopped, CPU and disk I/O normalized, and 6 poisoned orders/s now land in the DLQ for replay after the worker bug is fixed.

Reasoning: A quorum queue goes unavailable when it loses a majority of its Raft members. One node in maintenance should still leave 2 of 3, so first confirm what the cluster thinks is running.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_cluster_status|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-1"}
System Response: 3 disk nodes: rabbit@mq-1, rabbit@mq-2, rabbit@mq-3 — all running, no partitions, no alarms.
Recovery reasoning: Cluster membership is fine, but the queue's own Raft membership may not match the cluster. Check the queue object.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_get|> {"path": "/api/queues/%2F/payments.ledger"}
System Response: state: running, type: quorum, messages: 128441, leader: rabbit@mq-2, idle_since: 09:14:07.
Recovery reasoning: That "running" is not live truth — idle_since 09:14 predates the 09:32 maintenance window, so the stats DB is serving the queue's last pre-maintenance sample. Ask the Raft layer directly.
<|call:rabbitmq_queues_quorum_status|> {"vhost": "/", "queue": "payments.ledger"}
System Response: members: rabbit@mq-2 (offline, maintenance drain), rabbit@mq-1 (online, follower), rabbit@mq-old-2 (offline, node not in cluster). No leader — 1 of 3 members online.
Recovery reasoning: There is the loss: membership still includes rabbit@mq-old-2, decommissioned in March and never removed with shrink. With mq-2 stuck in maintenance drain that leaves 1 of 3 online — below majority. mq-2 rebooted but never exited maintenance mode; revive it to restore quorum.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_revive|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-2"}
System Response: rabbit@mq-2 revived, maintenance mode ended. payments.ledger: leader elected on rabbit@mq-1, 2 of 3 members online, queue available.
Recovery reasoning: Available again, but membership still counts a dead node, so the next single-node event repeats this outage. Remove the ghost member now.
<|call:rabbitmq_queues_shrink|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-old-2"}
System Response: rabbit@mq-old-2 removed from 14 quorum queues including payments.ledger; membership now [mq-1, mq-2]; queue healthy, backlog 128441 draining.
Resolution: Root cause: payments.ledger's quorum membership still contained decommissioned node mq-old-2, so mq-2's maintenance (which never auto-revived) dropped the queue to 1 of 3 members and quorum was lost. Revive plus shrink restored availability — publishes confirm and consumers deliver — and a grow onto mq-3 is scheduled tonight to return to three live replicas.

Reasoning: Confirms acked but events missing downstream means loss inside the broker path: an unbound routing key or a queue overflow policy. Check bindings first — unroutable messages are dropped silently even with confirms.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_bindings|> {"vhost": "/core", "exchange": "audit.topic"}
System Response: audit.topic -> audit.events, routing key audit.#, args {}. Single binding, matches all published keys.
Recovery reasoning: Routing is intact, so messages reach audit.events. Next rule out silent failures on the consumer side.
<|call:tail_app_logs|> {"service": "audit-consumer", "lines": 300}
System Response: 300 lines, zero errors; steady "event persisted seq=..." entries; consumer lag alerts all green.
Recovery reasoning: A clean consumer log invites closing this as an upstream bug, but one number refuses to fit: the queue has sat at exactly 50,000 ready for six hours. Real backlogs wander; a depth pinned to a round number is a ceiling, not a steady state. Check the queue's declared limits and rate arithmetic.
<|call:inspect_queue_args|> {"vhost": "/core", "queue": "audit.events"}
System Response: x-max-length=50000, x-overflow not set (default drop-head). Current rates: publish in 320/s, deliver 110/s, ready 50000 flat.
Recovery reasoning: 320/s in minus 110/s out with zero growth means 210/s are being discarded, and drop-head evicts the OLDEST messages first — exactly the block-shaped sequence gaps compliance found. First stop the silent loss by making overflow reject new publishes so producers retry from their buffers.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_set_policy|> {"vhost": "/core", "name": "audit-overflow", "pattern": "^audit.events$", "definition": {"overflow": "reject-publish"}, "apply-to": "queues"}
System Response: policy applied; producers now receive basic.nack while the queue is at max-length and hold events in retry buffers; drops stopped.
Recovery reasoning: Loss is stopped but the queue is still full at 50,000 — deliver must outrun the 320/s inflow to drain it. Scale the consumer fleet.
<|call:restart_consumer_fleet|> {"service": "audit-consumer", "replicas": 12}
System Response: 12 replicas up (was 4); deliver 110/s -> 360/s; ready 50000 and falling; producer retry buffers replaying nacked events in order.
Resolution: Root cause: audit.events was declared with x-max-length=50000 and default drop-head overflow, so at 320/s ingest the broker silently discarded the oldest 210 events/s while publish confirms kept acking. reject-publish ended the silent loss, the scaled fleet drains at a 40/s surplus, and replay from producer buffers restored contiguous sequence numbers.

Reasoning: Confirms acking 100% while specific tenants get nothing points at routability: a confirm only means the broker accepted the message — without the mandatory flag an unroutable message is confirmed and then dropped. Compare exchange in/out rates.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_get|> {"path": "/api/exchanges/%2Fnotify/notify.direct"}
System Response: message_stats: publish_in 850/s, publish_out 791/s. No error counters. Confirms 850/s acked.
Recovery reasoning: A dashboard reads 791-of-850 as sampling jitter, but these counters are exact: a steady 59/s gap between publish_in and publish_out is messages matching no binding, confirmed and discarded. That fits "new tenants only" if their bindings were never created. Check a fresh tenant's key.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_bindings|> {"vhost": "/notify", "exchange": "notify.direct", "routing_key": "tenant-4471"}
System Response: no bindings for routing key tenant-4471. Binding count on notify.direct: 3208; newest binding created Monday 02:14.
Recovery reasoning: No binding for a tenant onboarded Tuesday and none created after Monday 02:14 — the onboarding job that declares per-tenant queues and bindings has been dead since then. Capture unroutable traffic first so nothing more is lost; an alternate exchange does this without touching publisher code.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_set_policy|> {"vhost": "/notify", "name": "notify-ae", "pattern": "^notify.direct$", "definition": {"alternate-exchange": "notify.unrouted"}, "apply-to": "exchanges"}
System Response: policy applied; fanout notify.unrouted (bound to queue notify.unrouted.hold) receiving 59/s; publish_out plus AE traffic = 850/s = publish_in.
Recovery reasoning: The arithmetic closes, so loss has stopped. Now restart the dead onboarding worker so the ~40 missing tenant bindings get declared and held messages can be replayed.
<|call:restart_consumer_fleet|> {"service": "tenant-onboarding-worker"}
System Response: worker restarted; 41 queued onboarding jobs processed; 41 tenant bindings created; notify.unrouted.hold drained via replay; unrouted rate 0/s.
Resolution: Root cause: the tenant-onboarding worker died Monday 02:14, leaving new tenants without bindings on notify.direct; publisher confirms still acked because confirms do not guarantee routability, and without mandatory or an alternate exchange the broker silently dropped 59 msg/s. The AE policy captured the stream, onboarding replay restored bindings, and new-tenant delivery is verified end to end.

Reasoning: A hard failure edge at ~30 minutes on otherwise healthy workers suggests a timeout, not a crash. Read what the workers report when a long job dies.
<|call:tail_app_logs|> {"service": "transcode-worker", "grep": "channel|error", "lines": 100}
System Response: repeated: "AMQP channel 1 closed: PRECONDITION_FAILED - delivery acknowledgement on channel 1 timed out. Timeout value used: 1800000 ms" then "reconnected, consuming".
Recovery reasoning: The broker's consumer_timeout closes any channel that holds a delivery unacked past the limit, and 1800000 ms is the 30-minute default; the requeued message then lands on another worker — hence duplicates. Confirm the consumer state on the broker.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_consumers|> {"vhost": "/media", "queue": "media.transcode"}
System Response: 24 consumers, all active, prefetch_count=1, ack_required=true. Roster full, nothing abnormal.
Recovery reasoning: A full, active roster reads as "consumers are fine", but the consumer tags give it away: worker-07 holds ctag c419 where an hour ago it held c407 — twelve generations of tags on one pod. The snapshot hides constant channel death and rebirth that matches the timeout log line. Longest legitimate render is 55 min, so raise consumer_timeout well above it.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_eval|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-media-1", "expr": "application:set_env(rabbit, consumer_timeout, 7200000)."}
System Response: ok — consumer_timeout now 7200000 ms; applies to channels opened from now on; existing channels keep the old value.
Recovery reasoning: Only new channels inherit the 2-hour limit, so cycle the fleet once to reopen every channel under the new setting; the value is also being persisted to rabbitmq.conf so a broker restart cannot regress it.
<|call:restart_consumer_fleet|> {"service": "transcode-worker", "reason": "reopen channels under consumer_timeout=7200000"}
System Response: 24 workers restarted; channels reopened; redelivered rate 45/s -> 0.2/s over 20 min; three long renders (38-55 min) completed without channel loss.
Resolution: Root cause: the default 30-minute consumer_timeout closed the channel of any worker still mid-render, requeueing its delivery and duplicating work while instant reconnects kept the consumer roster looking healthy. Raising the timeout to 2h and cycling channels let long renders ack normally; redeliveries are near zero and GPU spend is back to baseline.

Reasoning: Slow memory growth under flat traffic points at object accumulation rather than message backlog. Get the broker's memory breakdown and object totals first.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_get|> {"path": "/api/overview"}
System Response: queues 214, messages 8900 total, publish 1.2k/s steady. Objects: connections 221, channels 84312. Memory breakdown: other_proc 61% and rising.
Recovery reasoning: Tiny backlog and steady rates make this look like a beam or OS-level leak worth escalating to platform — but the object counts refuse that story: 84,312 channels on 221 connections is 381 channels per connection, where sane clients hold single digits. Every channel is an Erlang process, which is exactly what other_proc growth means. Find the owner.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_connections|> {"columns": ["client", "channels"], "sort": "channels", "limit": 5}
System Response: 12 connections from payments-worker hold 6,890-6,930 channels each; every other connection holds 8 or fewer.
Recovery reasoning: The leak is entirely payments-worker. Inspect its channels to see how they are created and abandoned.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_channels|> {"connection_filter": "payments-worker", "columns": ["number", "consumer_count", "messages_unacknowledged", "idle_since"], "limit": 10}
System Response: sampled channels: consumer_count=0, unacked=0, idle since creation; channel numbers increment steadily — one new channel every ~5s per connection.
Recovery reasoning: Idle, consumerless channels born every 5 seconds match payments-worker's 5-second billing tick: the app opens a channel per publish and never closes it. Their last merge replaced the shared channel pool with a naive wrapper. Roll the fleet onto the hotfix that restores pooling.
<|call:restart_consumer_fleet|> {"service": "payments-worker", "image": "payments-worker:v8.3.1-channelpool"}
System Response: 12 pods restarted; channels 84312 -> 259 within 2 min; other_proc memory released; node memory 71% -> 44%; management UI responsive.
Resolution: Root cause: payments-worker v8.3.0 dropped its channel pool and opened a new AMQP channel on every 5-second publish tick without closing it, leaking ~6,900 channels per connection and inflating other_proc memory toward the alarm threshold. The pooling hotfix collapsed the count to 259; memory has been flat for the past hour.

Reasoning: High CPU with flat connections and modest message rates means the broker is doing expensive non-messaging work — TLS handshakes and connection setup are prime suspects. Check node counters.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_status|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-prod-2"}
System Response: fd used 3890/4096, sockets 3710, CPU 87%. No alarms. Uptime 41 days.
Recovery reasoning: fd usage near the ceiling with only ~400 steady connections means descriptors are consumed and released fast — churn the 30s-sampled panel cannot see. Confirm with the churn counters.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_get|> {"path": "/api/overview", "fields": "churn_rates"}
System Response: connection_created 46/s, connection_closed 46/s, channel_created 46/s. Queue and exchange churn ~0.
Recovery reasoning: 46 TLS connects per second explains the CPU. The 07:00 onset matches this morning's checkout-svc release, which the runbook names as the usual churn suspect. Pull its logs.
<|call:tail_app_logs|> {"service": "checkout-svc", "grep": "amqp", "lines": 120}
System Response: connections established at boot, heartbeats every 30s, no reconnect entries; 6 long-lived connections per pod, stable for hours.
Recovery reasoning: Blaming checkout-svc was the runbook talking, not the data: its connections are long-lived, and the broker's churny client identifies as product=py-amqp while checkout runs the Java client — the library fingerprint clears it. Group live connections by client properties to find the real source.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_connections|> {"columns": ["client_properties.product", "peer_host", "connected_at"], "group_by": "client_properties.product"}
System Response: py-amqp: 388 connections, all with connected_at inside the last 12 seconds; peer hosts are metrics-exporter DaemonSet pods — 694 nodes scraping every 15s with a fresh connection per scrape.
Recovery reasoning: 694 nodes / 15s is the 46 connects per second, each paying a TLS handshake and an fd. Ship the exporter change to hold one persistent connection per pod.
<|call:deploy_config_change|> {"service": "metrics-exporter", "change": "reuse persistent AMQP connection across scrapes", "rollout": "daemonset"}
System Response: rollout complete; connection_created 46/s -> 0.3/s; fd used 3890 -> 1240; CPU 87% -> 24%; zero accept failures for 30 min.
Resolution: Root cause: the metrics-exporter DaemonSet opened a fresh TLS AMQP connection on every 15-second scrape across 694 nodes — 46 connects/s that saturated CPU with handshakes and churned fds to the limit while the sampled connection panel stayed flat. Persistent connections removed the churn; CPU, fds, and accepts are back to baseline.

Reasoning: A shovel that stopped moving messages while both clusters are healthy is usually a broken link — auth or TLS on the destination side. Check the shovel's status first.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_get|> {"path": "/api/shovels/%2Forders"}
System Response: orders-to-dr: state running, src orders.outbound, dest amqps://mq-dr:5671, last_changed 09:44:03.
Recovery reasoning: "running" would close the ticket — except last_changed is eight seconds ago on a shovel defined months back. That timestamp is when the supervisor last restarted the worker: a crash-looping shovel reports running on every poll because it is always freshly restarted. The truth is in the broker log.
<|call:tail_broker_logs|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-prod-1", "grep": "shovel", "lines": 60}
System Response: every ~10s since 06:12:41: "Shovel 'orders-to-dr' failed to connect: TLS alert: unknown_ca" then "restarting in 10s".
Recovery reasoning: The destination cert can no longer be verified against the CA bundle pinned in the shovel URI — and mq-dr rotated its certificate onto the new internal CA at 06:10, two minutes before the first failure. Update the shovel parameter to trust the new chain.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_put|> {"path": "/api/parameters/shovel/%2Forders/orders-to-dr", "body": {"value": {"src-uri": "amqp://", "src-queue": "orders.outbound", "dest-uri": "amqps://mq-dr:5671?cacertfile=/etc/rabbitmq/ca-2026.pem"}}}
System Response: parameter updated; shovel restarted with new definition; connected to mq-dr; state running with zero restarts for 120s.
Recovery reasoning: Link is stable. Verify the backlog is actually draining rather than trusting the state field again.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_queues|> {"vhost": "/orders", "columns": ["name", "messages", "message_stats.deliver_rate"], "filter": "orders.outbound"}
System Response: orders.outbound: 148000 -> 96400 and falling at ~2.1k/s; DR-side queue confirms matching ingest.
Resolution: Root cause: mq-dr's TLS certificate was rotated to a new internal CA at 06:10 while the shovel URI pinned the old CA bundle, so orders-to-dr crash-looped on unknown_ca for hours — invisibly, because the supervisor's constant restarts kept its status reading "running". Updating cacertfile in the shovel parameter restored the link; the 148k backlog cleared in under two minutes and replication is current.

Reasoning: A TTL-plus-DLX delay queue with late expiries either has a broken dead-letter path or messages not expiring on schedule. Start with the queue's live shape.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_queues|> {"vhost": "/orders", "columns": ["name", "messages", "message_stats.publish_rate", "message_stats.dlx_rate"], "filter": "orders.retry.30s"}
System Response: orders.retry.30s: messages 3100, publish in 40/s, dead-letter out 39/s.
Recovery reasoning: In and out are balanced so the DLX path works. Check the management view for message age.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_get|> {"path": "/api/queues/%2Forders/orders.retry.30s"}
System Response: avg age of recently expired messages: 31s. State running, memory stable, all panels nominal.
Recovery reasoning: A 31-second average says on-SLA, but Little's law disagrees: 3,100 resident messages at 40/s inflow means the average message actually spends 77 seconds in the queue. The panel averages only messages that recently expired — survivors stuck inside never enter it. Per-message TTL is only enforced at the queue head, so something long-lived must be parked there. Sample the head.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_post|> {"path": "/api/queues/%2Forders/orders.retry.30s/get", "body": {"count": 3, "requeue": true, "encoding": "auto"}}
System Response: msg1: expiration=1800000, app-id=bulk-reconciler, published 13:21; msg2: expiration=30000, published 13:52; msg3: expiration=30000, published 13:52.
Recovery reasoning: There is the dam: bulk-reconciler began publishing 30-minute-TTL messages into the 30-second queue at 13:21, and since expiry only fires at the head, each one blocks every 30s message behind it for up to 30 minutes — matching the observed lag. When both queue-level and per-message TTL are set the lower wins, so a queue-level cap of 30s makes the dams expire on time too.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_set_policy|> {"vhost": "/orders", "name": "retry30s-cap", "pattern": "^orders.retry.30s$", "definition": {"message-ttl": 30000}, "apply-to": "queues"}
System Response: policy applied. Depth 3100 -> 240 over 3 min; dead-letter out spiked to 310/s then settled at 40/s; retry latency p99 30.8s.
Resolution: Root cause: bulk-reconciler misrouted 1800000 ms TTL messages into orders.retry.30s, and because per-message TTL only expires at the queue head, each one head-blocked the 30-second retries queued behind it. The queue-level message-ttl=30000 policy caps effective TTL at the lower value, restoring p99 retry latency to ~31s; bulk-reconciler is being repointed at a dedicated orders.retry.30m queue.

Reasoning: All publishers blocking at once across vhosts while consumers run points to a resource alarm. Verify the node in front of me first.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_status|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-prod-1"}
System Response: rabbit@mq-prod-1: disk_free 92GiB (limit 48MiB), mem 2.9/6.4GiB, no local alarms. Node healthy.
Recovery reasoning: A clean local status makes it tempting to chase the load balancer or the apps — but resource alarms are cluster-wide by design: an alarm raised on ANY node blocks publishers on EVERY node, so a healthy local node proves nothing. List alarms across all members.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_cluster_status|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-prod-1"}
System Response: 3 nodes running. Alarms: [{disk, rabbit@mq-prod-3}]. Network partitions: none.
Recovery reasoning: mq-prod-3 tripped its free-disk-limit alarm and is blocking publishing for the whole cluster. See how bad it is and what ate the disk.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_status|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-prod-3"}
System Response: disk_free 41MiB (limit 48MiB), alarms: [disk]. Largest path under /var/lib/rabbitmq: log/rabbit@mq-prod-3.log at 88GiB, debug level, growing ~0.5GiB/hour since 2026-06-26.
Recovery reasoning: Debug logging left on after last week's incident grew the broker log to 88GiB and starved the data partition. Rotate and compress it, and drop the level back to info so it stays fixed.
<|call:rotate_and_compress_logs|> {"node": "rabbit@mq-prod-3", "set_log_level": "info"}
System Response: rabbit@mq-prod-3.log rotated and compressed (88GiB -> 3.1GiB archive); log level info; disk_free 84.7GiB; disk alarm cleared 03:58; connection.unblocked sent to 402 connections.
Recovery reasoning: Alarm cleared — confirm publishers actually resumed cluster-wide rather than trusting the alarm state alone.
<|call:rabbitmq_mgmt_api_get|> {"path": "/api/overview", "fields": "message_stats"}
System Response: publish 5.8k/s (baseline 5.6k/s plus catch-up burst), 0 blocked connections; checkout, ingest, and telemetry alerts recovered.
Resolution: Root cause: debug logging left enabled on mq-prod-3 since 2026-06-26 grew its log to 88GiB, pushing disk_free below the 48MiB limit; the disk alarm on that single node blocked all publishers cluster-wide while consumers kept draining. Rotation plus resetting the log level cleared the alarm and publishing recovered on every vhost.

Reasoning: Near-zero visible depth with a 41-minute p99 means jobs wait somewhere the depth panel cannot see — most likely pre-assigned into consumer buffers. Split ready from unacked.
<|call:rabbitmqctl_list_queues|> {"vhost": "/media", "columns": ["name", "messages_ready", "messages_unacknowledged", "consumers"], "filter": "jobs.render"}
System Response: jobs.render: ready=2, unacked=347, consumers=20.
Recovery reasoning: 347 jobs already delivered but unfinished — the real backlog lives inside worker buffers, invisible to the depth graph. Trace one slow job's assignment.
